## Code Example
```javascript
// Deterministic mock setup for Playwright routing
page.route('https://auth.example.com/**', route => {
  const tenantId = 'tenant-' + Date.now();
  route.fulfill({
    status: 200,
    body: JSON.stringify({ tenantId, status: 'provisioned' })
  });
});

// Feature flag toggle for onboarding-v2
await context.addInitScript(() => {
  window.__FEATURE_FLAGS__ = { 'onboarding-v2': true };
});

// Retry with exponential backoff for eventual consistency
async function waitForRegionSync(tenantId, region, maxRetries = 5) {
  for (let i = 0; i < maxRetries; i++) {
    const response = await api.get(`/tenants/${tenantId}/status?region=${region}`);
    if (response.status === 'synced') return true;
    await sleep(Math.pow(2, i) * 1000);
  }
  throw new Error('Region sync timeout');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests that exercise keyboard accessibility demonstrate familiarity with a11y concepts and deterministic testing approaches for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Keyboard navigation and focus management
- ARIA roles and attributes (role=dialog, aria-label, aria-live)
- Test determinism with seeded localStorage and deterministic delays

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test outline in Cypress
describe('Keyboard accessibility for notes', () => {
  it('navigates via Tab, opens modal, saves, edits, deletes, and persists', () => {
    // seed
    window.localStorage.setItem('notes', JSON.stringify([
      { id: 1, title: 'Note 1', body: 'Body 1' },
      { id: 2, title: 'Note 2', body: 'Body 2' }
    ]));

    cy.visit('/');

    // Tab through list and verify focus
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-item"]').first().focus();
    cy.focused().should('have.attr', 'role', 'listitem');

    // Open create modal with keyboard
    cy.get('[data-testid="create-button"]').focus().type('{enter}');
    cy.get('[role="dialog"]').should('be.visible');

    // Create note
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-title"]').type('New Note');
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-body"]').type('New Body');
    cy.get('[data-testid="save-button"]').click();

    // Edit existing note
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-item"]').first().focus().type('{enter}');
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-title"]').clear().type('Edited Title');
    cy.get('[data-testid="save-button"]').click();

    // Delete note
    cy.get('[data-testid="note-item"]').first().focus();
    cy.get('[data-testid="delete-button"]').click();

    // Verify persistence after reload
    cy.reload();
    cy.window().then(win => {
      const notes = JSON.parse(win.localStorage.getItem('notes'));
      expect(notes).to.have.length(2);
    });
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical E2E testing skills for beginners: data seeding, mocking remote services (feature flags), validating UI variants, and ensuring idempotent events within a fintech onboarding flow.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and remote config
- Test data seeding and isolation
- API interception/mocking
- Idempotent event verification
- Variant-driven UI testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for onboarding with feature flag
describe('Onboarding feature flag', () => {
  const userId = 'test-user-001';
  beforeEach(() => {
    cy.request('POST', '/test/setup-user', { userId });
  });

  it('Variant A renders steps A1/A2', () => {
    cy.intercept('/api/flags', { variant: 'A' }).as('flagA');
    cy.visit('/onboarding');
    cy.wait('@flagA');
    cy.get('.step1').should('be.visible');
  });

  it('Idempotent event when flag toggled', () => {
    cy.intercept('/api/flags', { variant: 'A' }).as('flagA');
    cy.visit('/onboarding');
    // complete some steps
    cy.reload(); // simulate flag refresh
    cy.intercept('/api/flags', { variant: 'B' }).as('flagB');
    cy.wait('@flagB');
    cy.get('@onlyOnboardingEvent').should('have.length', 1);
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to more than two variants and ensure coverage of each path?
- How would you integrate this with CI to catch flakey flag-based flows?
